      title: "When an Idea’s Time Has Come: ideas are everywhere, courage to act is rare",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 1998)", section: "Chapter 51 · When an Idea’s Time Has Come · pp. 114–115", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "There is a time for every idea, and when that time comes, the idea springs into the minds of several people simultaneously.", page: "114" },
        { text: "Though the world is overflowing with good ideas, people with the courage to act are rare.", page: "114" }
      ],
      idea: "Roy opens with a tongue-in-cheek rival to Einstein: his own **Theory of Universal Knowledge**. Every idea has its moment, and when that moment arrives the idea lands in several heads at once. The person who acts first gets the applause. Everyone else sits back and complains that he stole their idea.\n\nHis proof is the light bulb. Two inventors, Swan and Sawyer, are working on opposite sides of the Atlantic on the same plan Edison has: run a current through a filament inside a vacuum. Both of them are **further along** in their experiments than Edison. But Edison is the one who publicly announces he can do it, more than a year before he actually shows electric light. Edison gets the fame and the fortune because he had the nerve to make the claim. Swan and Sawyer had the better lab results and kept quiet.\n\nRoy then explains why this keeps happening more and more. When millions of people take in the same news, the same shows and the same information, it should surprise nobody that many of them reach the same idea at about the same time. Talent and even genius are now common. He bets that you have enough talent to be world-famous for something. So talent is not the question. The question is whether you will act on it or leave it in the ground like unmined gold.\n\nThe chapter ends on a spiritual note, which is typical of Part Two's closing chapters. If you were made in the image of a Creator, maybe you were built to do great things and then handed the choice. Maybe your life so far has prepared you for this exact moment (an echo of the Book of Esther). The last line is simply: *do you have the courage to act?*\n\n**What Roy really means:** an idea on its own is worth almost nothing, because other people almost certainly have it too. What's worth something is the **public commitment plus action**. Edison didn't win the science; he won the claim, and then he did the work to back it up. Read this together with the next chapter (52), where Roy warns that acting too early can be just as fatal. The two chapters are a pair: move fast when the time has come, and test honestly whether it has. Edison shows up again in Chapters 63 and 64, which tells you how much Roy admires him as a *doer*.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Roy's Theory of Universal Knowledge: when an idea's time comes, it shows up in several minds at once.",
        "The first person to act publicly gets the credit, not the person with the best private progress (Edison vs. Swan and Sawyer).",
        "Mass media gives millions of people the same inputs, so the same ideas appear at once more and more often.",
        "Talent is common; the scarce ingredient is the courage to commit out loud and then act.",
        "Making a claim works only if you're willing to do the work that backs it up. Edison delivered."
      ],
      why: "Every agency owner in 2026 watches the same YouTube breakdowns, uses the same AI tools and reads the same Meta updates, so whatever Zach is thinking about doing, someone in Sacramento is thinking about it too. In each local niche, the agency that announces the offer and books the calls first ends up owning it.",
      example: {
        title: "Claiming the idea before someone else does",
        before: "Zach notices that almost no Sacramento roofers run short “here's what we found on your roof” video ads. He writes it in his notes app: “Roofer video ad program, someday, when I have a case study.” Three months later a competitor posts that exact offer on LinkedIn and signs two roofers.",
        after: "Same week he notices it, Zach posts: “I'm opening the Sacramento Roofer Video Ad Program. I'll film one inspection with you, turn it into 3 ads, and run them for 30 days. Taking 3 roofers this month.” Then he sends 15 DMs to roofers from his list.",
        body: "Like Edison, Zach makes the claim before he's finished, but only a claim he knows he can deliver: he can film, edit and run ads. The public post creates accountability and makes him the person associated with the idea. The DMs are the action that keeps the claim honest."
      },
