      idea: "The third Vanderbilt chapter goes back to the man who made the fortune, Willie II's great-grandfather.\n\n**Cornelius Vanderbilt** was in first grade the year George Washington died. He quit school at eleven. At sixteen he borrowed his mother's savings, bought a small sailboat and started ferrying people and cargo from Staten Island to New York City. Driven, disciplined and efficient, he soon owned the route and broke his competitors (Roy doesn't pretend he was gentle). The little boat business became the **Staten Island Ferry**. By forty his ships served ports along the whole Atlantic coast, earning him the name “Commodore.”\n\nSince passengers and freight were his business, buying struggling railroads and turning them around was a natural next step. The difference from the previous owners: **his trains ran on time and the service was excellent.** The New York Central became the largest enterprise in the country. During the Panic of 1873 he gave thousands of people jobs by building **Grand Central Station**.\n\nThen comes Roy's twist: better service and lower prices are *not* what made Vanderbilt rich. A friend described the traits that did. He was the largest employer in the country, yet he:\n\n- despised routine office work,\n- kept his figures in a **vest-pocket notebook**,\n- ate sparingly,\n- **never speculated in stocks**,\n- **never refused to see a caller**,\n- rose early,\n- read *Pilgrim's Progress* every year,\n- and relaxed by playing whist and driving his trotting horses.\n\nRoy's conclusion: Vanderbilt didn't offer better service and lower prices *in order to* get rich. He got rich because **he loved hauling passengers and freight and did it very, very well.** Why do you do what you do? Wealth isn't a destination or a shining city on a hill. It's a **by-product of passion**. The sidebar (Ben Sweetland): success is a journey, not a destination.\n\n**Reading the three chapters together:** Willie Sr. had money and no dream, and he was miserable. Willie II had a dream and fascination, and he was joyful. Cornelius had a passion for his trade, and the fortune was the side effect. The friend's list is worth studying as an operating system: simple numbers, discipline, no gambling outside your craft (compare Tony's speculation in Chapter 59), total accessibility to customers, yearly reflection and real play.",
