      idea: "Roy tells a true story in two halves: a teenage mother and the son she raised.\n\n**The setup.** Sue leaves school at sixteen, pregnant, with no education, skills, money or support. What she chooses *not* to teach her son is telling: no lectures about schooling, no warnings about falling in love young. She gives him only two messages:\n\n1. **The world is looking for people it can depend on, so be one of them.**\n2. Repeated every morning as he heads off to school: you're smart, things come easily to you, you can be anything, and **opportunity is all around you.**\n\nThen she goes off to an entry-level filing job.\n\n**The boy** is an average student. He tries college on a government grant, gets bored, comes home and marries young, still believing he's smart and that opportunity is everywhere.\n\n**Sue** turns out to be the person her employers can always **depend** on, and she sees **solutions where others see only problems** (the same trait Roy praised in Richard Sears, Chapter 68). She rises step by step and becomes the company's fixer: each department that falls into chaos gets handed to her to reorganize and remotivate, until she has run every one of them. She retires wealthy at fifty-four and starts modeling, to her friends' amusement, with a sharp insight: women her age want to see clothes on someone shaped like them, not on twenty-year-olds. Agencies turn her away, she keeps going, and she ends up modeling on TV, runways and in catalogs in America and Europe.\n\n**The payoff:** her son now earns more than the president, because she taught him to see opportunity everywhere and people learned they could depend on him. Roy insists the story is true and that sad beginnings don't require sad endings. He never names the boy, so read the clues and decide for yourself. The sidebar (Doc Sane) says opportunity is as plentiful as oxygen and just as unnoticed.\n\n**Three lessons:**\n1. **Dependability** is the scarce quality that got both mother and son promoted and paid.\n2. **What you tell yourself daily becomes the lens you see with.** Sue installed a belief, and the belief found the opportunities.\n3. **Sue's modeling insight is a sharp marketing insight:** customers want to see someone *like them* using the product. That pairs with Chapter 18's Walter Mitty: show people who look like your customers doing the brave thing your customers secretly want to do.",
